---
title: Structured Outputs With Tools
id: structured-outputs-with-tools
order: 5
description: "Combine outputSchema with tools so the agent loop runs first (calling tools as needed) and then returns a validated typed object. Includes the pause / resume flow for tool-approval gates and client-tool invocations inside a structured run."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - structured outputs
  - tools
  - agent loop
  - tool approval
  - client tools
  - outputSchema tools
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want the agent to use tools to gather information, then return a structured object summarizing what it found. \"Recommend a product for a developer\" → the loop calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getProductPrice"},{"type":"text","value":", hits an inventory API, then returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ productName, currentPrice, reason }"},{"type":"text","value":" validated against your schema. The structured response only fires after every tool resolves."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This page covers the combined "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools"},{"type":"text","value":" shape, including the pause/resume points (server-tool approval prompts, client-tool invocations) that can land mid-run before the structured object arrives."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On adapters that support native combined mode (modern OpenAI, Claude 4.5+, Gemini 3.x, Grok 4.x, and "},{"type":"link","href":"../adapters/openrouter.md#tools-and-structured-output-together","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenRouter on those same upstream models"}]},{"type":"text","value":"), "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ tools, outputSchema, stream: true })"},{"type":"text","value":" is one streaming request. The extra finalization call does not run."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The React chat example has a live OpenRouter page at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/generations/openrouter-combined"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" If you're not yet familiar with how tools work in TanStack AI, read "},{"type":"link","href":"../tools/tool-architecture","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Architecture"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"../tools/server-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" first. The patterns here build on the regular agent-loop flow — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outputSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" just adds a final terminal event."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"non-streaming-tools-first-then-structured-object","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-streaming: tools first, then structured object"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The simplest shape: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await chat({ tools, outputSchema })"},{"type":"text","value":". The agent loop runs to completion (every tool resolved, every approval responded to), then the model produces the structured object. The promise resolves with the validated, typed result."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst getProductPrice = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_product_price\",\n  description: \"Get the current price of a product\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ productId: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ productId }) => {\n  return { price: 29.99, currency: \"USD\" };\n});\n\nconst RecommendationSchema = z.object({\n  productName: z.string(),\n  currentPrice: z.number(),\n  reason: z.string(),\n});\n\nconst recommendation = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Recommend a product for a developer\" }],\n  tools: [getProductPrice],\n  outputSchema: RecommendationSchema,\n});\n\nrecommendation.productName;  // string — fully typed\nrecommendation.currentPrice; // number\nrecommendation.reason;       // string","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The agent decides when to call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get_product_price"},{"type":"text","value":", executes the tool, integrates the result into its reasoning, and only then produces the final structured response. You see the validated object; the tool calls happen behind the scenes."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Coding agents in a sandbox (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Grok Build) run native tools and the schema on the same turn. They do not pause for TanStack tool approval or client tools. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./harnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harness Agents"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"streaming-lifecycle-events-before-the-structured-payload","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming: lifecycle events before the structured payload"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream: true"},{"type":"text","value":" and the wire format changes — the client now sees tool-call events as they happen, "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"then"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the structured-output stream emits its terminal event. The lifecycle ordering is:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_STARTED"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(Agent loop) "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TOOL_CALL_START"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TOOL_CALL_ARGS"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TOOL_CALL_END"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT"},{"type":"text","value":", possibly repeating for multiple tool calls or iterations"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.start"},{"type":"text","value":" (once the model begins emitting the JSON response)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT"},{"type":"text","value":" deltas (the JSON itself)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" (completed payload)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":"'s "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" while step 2 is running — the structured stream hasn't started yet. Once step 3 fires, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" begins filling in; on step 5, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" snaps."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the separate-finalization path, the agent loop may also complete a plain-text assistant message before step 3. That message and the structured-output assistant message remain separate. Native-combined output keeps the structured JSON and its part on one assistant message."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The tool-call parts land on the assistant message exactly as they would in a normal streaming chat. Render them however you'd render tool calls outside a structured-output run."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-tools-that-need-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server tools that need approval"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A server tool registered with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval: true"},{"type":"text","value":" doesn't execute automatically — the agent loop pauses, the queued tool-call lands on the assistant message as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolCallPart"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state === \"approval-requested\""},{"type":"text","value":", and the loop waits for you to call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addToolApprovalResponse({ id, approved })"},{"type":"text","value":" from the hook return. The structured-output stream only takes over once approval is granted (or denied and the loop resumes)."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { RecommendationSchema } from \"./api/recommend\";\nimport { sendEmail } from \"./tools\";\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage, partial, final, addToolApprovalResponse } =\n  useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/recommend\"),\n    outputSchema: RecommendationSchema,\n    tools: [sendEmail], // server tool with needsApproval: true\n  });\n\nconst last = messages.at(-1);\n\nreturn (\n  <>\n    {last?.parts.map((part, i) => {\n      // Surface approval prompts inline.\n      if (\n        part.type === \"tool-call\" &&\n        part.state === \"approval-requested\" &&\n        part.approval\n      ) {\n        return (\n          <ApprovalPrompt\n            key={i}\n            part={part}\n            onApprove={() =>\n              addToolApprovalResponse({ id: part.approval!.id, approved: true })\n            }\n            onDeny={() =>\n              addToolApprovalResponse({ id: part.approval!.id, approved: false })\n            }\n          />\n        );\n      }\n      if (part.type === \"thinking\") return <ReasoningView key={i} text={part.content} />;\n      if (part.type === \"tool-call\") return <ToolCallView key={i} part={part} />;\n      return null;\n    })}\n\n    {/* The structured payload — fills in once tools resolve. */}\n    <StructuredView data={final ?? partial} />\n  </>\n);","lang":"tsx","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While the approval is pending, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" stays at its last value (which is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" on a first run) and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":". As soon as the user approves (or denies and the loop continues), the agent loop resumes, the structured stream runs, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" populate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The full server-tool approval pattern lives in "},{"type":"link","href":"../tools/tool-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Approval Flow"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The only structured-output-specific note: the approval can land "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"before"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the structured stream starts. That's why "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" while you're staring at an approval prompt — there's no JSON yet."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-tools-mid-run","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client tools mid-run"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client tools — defined with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client((input) => ...)"},{"type":"text","value":" on the tool definition — execute automatically when the model calls them. The server ends the current run with an internal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client-tool-execution"},{"type":"text","value":" interrupt that does not appear in the public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" array. The client runs the registered "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation and submits its output in a resume batch. Once every client tool resolves, the agent loop continues into the structured-output stream. There's no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onToolCall"},{"type":"text","value":" option to wire up on the hook side."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { runLookupOnClient } from \"./client-utils\";\nimport { RecommendationSchema } from \"./api/recommend\";\n\nconst lookupContactDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"lookup_contact\",\n  description: \"Find a contact by name\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ name: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ email: z.string(), phone: z.string() }),\n});\n\n// `.client()` registers the browser-side implementation. Calls land here\n// automatically when the model invokes the tool.\nconst lookupContact = lookupContactDef.client((input) => {\n  // Look up in local state, IndexedDB, an in-process address book, etc.\n  return runLookupOnClient(input);\n});\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage, partial, final } = useChat({\n  outputSchema: RecommendationSchema,\n  tools: [lookupContact],\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/recommend\"),\n});","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While the client tool runs, the agent loop is paused and the structured-output stream hasn't started — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{}"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":". As soon as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation returns, the loop resumes, the structured stream takes over, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":" populate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../tools/client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full pattern (typed inputs / outputs, multiple client tools, mixing with server tools, surfacing tool calls in the message renderer)."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"multi-turn-tools-structured-output","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-turn + tools + structured output"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composes naturally. Every turn runs the agent loop (with any tool gates), then produces a structured-output assistant message when the run completes successfully. The next turn sees the prior recipe (or recommendation, or report) as assistant content and can iterate on it. The separate-finalization path can also retain the agent loop's plain-text assistant message before that structured response."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The only thing to be careful of: between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sendMessage()"},{"type":"text","value":" and the first structured-output event, the latest turn has no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output"},{"type":"text","value":" part yet — your render loop's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"m.parts.find(p => p.type === \"structured-output\")"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":". Render a \"streaming…\" placeholder when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isLoading && messages[last]?.role === \"user\""},{"type":"text","value":" to cover that gap. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./multi-turn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Turn Chat"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full pattern."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"non-streaming-tools-first-then-structured-object","text":"Non-streaming: tools first, then structured object","level":2},{"id":"streaming-lifecycle-events-before-the-structured-payload","text":"Streaming: lifecycle events before the structured payload","level":2},{"id":"server-tools-that-need-approval","text":"Server tools that need approval","level":2},{"id":"client-tools-mid-run","text":"Client tools mid-run","level":2},{"id":"multi-turn-tools-structured-output","text":"Multi-turn + tools + structured output","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou want the agent to use tools to gather information, then return a structured object summarizing what it found. \"Recommend a product for a developer\" → the loop calls `getProductPrice`, hits an inventory API, then returns `{ productName, currentPrice, reason }` validated against your schema. The structured response only fires after every tool resolves.\n\nThis page covers the combined `outputSchema` + `tools` shape, including the pause/resume points (server-tool approval prompts, client-tool invocations) that can land mid-run before the structured object arrives.\n\nOn adapters that support native combined mode (modern OpenAI, Claude 4.5+, Gemini 3.x, Grok 4.x, and [OpenRouter on those same upstream models](../adapters/openrouter.md#tools-and-structured-output-together)), `chat({ tools, outputSchema, stream: true })` is one streaming request. The extra finalization call does not run.\n\nThe React chat example has a live OpenRouter page at `/generations/openrouter-combined`.\n\n> **Note:** If you're not yet familiar with how tools work in TanStack AI, read [Tool Architecture](../tools/tool-architecture) and [Server Tools](../tools/server-tools) first. The patterns here build on the regular agent-loop flow — `outputSchema` just adds a final terminal event.\n\n## Non-streaming: tools first, then structured object\n\nThe simplest shape: `await chat({ tools, outputSchema })`. The agent loop runs to completion (every tool resolved, every approval responded to), then the model produces the structured object. The promise resolves with the validated, typed result.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst getProductPrice = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_product_price\",\n  description: \"Get the current price of a product\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ productId: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ productId }) => {\n  return { price: 29.99, currency: \"USD\" };\n});\n\nconst RecommendationSchema = z.object({\n  productName: z.string(),\n  currentPrice: z.number(),\n  reason: z.string(),\n});\n\nconst recommendation = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Recommend a product for a developer\" }],\n  tools: [getProductPrice],\n  outputSchema: RecommendationSchema,\n});\n\nrecommendation.productName;  // string — fully typed\nrecommendation.currentPrice; // number\nrecommendation.reason;       // string\n```\n\nThe agent decides when to call `get_product_price`, executes the tool, integrates the result into its reasoning, and only then produces the final structured response. You see the validated object; the tool calls happen behind the scenes.\n\n> **Note:** Coding agents in a sandbox (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Grok Build) run native tools and the schema on the same turn. They do not pause for TanStack tool approval or client tools. See [Harness Agents](./harnesses).\n\n## Streaming: lifecycle events before the structured payload\n\nPass `stream: true` and the wire format changes — the client now sees tool-call events as they happen, _then_ the structured-output stream emits its terminal event. The lifecycle ordering is:\n\n1. `RUN_STARTED`\n2. (Agent loop) `TOOL_CALL_START` → `TOOL_CALL_ARGS` → `TOOL_CALL_END` → `TOOL_CALL_RESULT`, possibly repeating for multiple tool calls or iterations\n3. `structured-output.start` (once the model begins emitting the JSON response)\n4. `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` deltas (the JSON itself)\n5. `structured-output.complete` (completed payload)\n6. `RUN_FINISHED`\n\n`useChat`'s `partial` stays `{}` and `final` stays `null` while step 2 is running — the structured stream hasn't started yet. Once step 3 fires, `partial` begins filling in; on step 5, `final` snaps.\n\nOn the separate-finalization path, the agent loop may also complete a plain-text assistant message before step 3. That message and the structured-output assistant message remain separate. Native-combined output keeps the structured JSON and its part on one assistant message.\n\nThe tool-call parts land on the assistant message exactly as they would in a normal streaming chat. Render them however you'd render tool calls outside a structured-output run.\n\n## Server tools that need approval\n\nA server tool registered with `needsApproval: true` doesn't execute automatically — the agent loop pauses, the queued tool-call lands on the assistant message as a `ToolCallPart` with `state === \"approval-requested\"`, and the loop waits for you to call `addToolApprovalResponse({ id, approved })` from the hook return. The structured-output stream only takes over once approval is granted (or denied and the loop resumes).\n\n```tsx ignore\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { RecommendationSchema } from \"./api/recommend\";\nimport { sendEmail } from \"./tools\";\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage, partial, final, addToolApprovalResponse } =\n  useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/recommend\"),\n    outputSchema: RecommendationSchema,\n    tools: [sendEmail], // server tool with needsApproval: true\n  });\n\nconst last = messages.at(-1);\n\nreturn (\n  <>\n    {last?.parts.map((part, i) => {\n      // Surface approval prompts inline.\n      if (\n        part.type === \"tool-call\" &&\n        part.state === \"approval-requested\" &&\n        part.approval\n      ) {\n        return (\n          <ApprovalPrompt\n            key={i}\n            part={part}\n            onApprove={() =>\n              addToolApprovalResponse({ id: part.approval!.id, approved: true })\n            }\n            onDeny={() =>\n              addToolApprovalResponse({ id: part.approval!.id, approved: false })\n            }\n          />\n        );\n      }\n      if (part.type === \"thinking\") return <ReasoningView key={i} text={part.content} />;\n      if (part.type === \"tool-call\") return <ToolCallView key={i} part={part} />;\n      return null;\n    })}\n\n    {/* The structured payload — fills in once tools resolve. */}\n    <StructuredView data={final ?? partial} />\n  </>\n);\n```\n\nWhile the approval is pending, `partial` stays at its last value (which is `{}` on a first run) and `final` stays `null`. As soon as the user approves (or denies and the loop continues), the agent loop resumes, the structured stream runs, and `partial` / `final` populate.\n\nThe full server-tool approval pattern lives in [Tool Approval Flow](../tools/tool-approval). The only structured-output-specific note: the approval can land **before** the structured stream starts. That's why `partial` reads `{}` while you're staring at an approval prompt — there's no JSON yet.\n\n## Client tools mid-run\n\nClient tools — defined with `.client((input) => ...)` on the tool definition — execute automatically when the model calls them. The server ends the current run with an internal `client-tool-execution` interrupt that does not appear in the public `interrupts` array. The client runs the registered `.client()` implementation and submits its output in a resume batch. Once every client tool resolves, the agent loop continues into the structured-output stream. There's no `onToolCall` option to wire up on the hook side.\n\n```tsx\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { runLookupOnClient } from \"./client-utils\";\nimport { RecommendationSchema } from \"./api/recommend\";\n\nconst lookupContactDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"lookup_contact\",\n  description: \"Find a contact by name\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ name: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ email: z.string(), phone: z.string() }),\n});\n\n// `.client()` registers the browser-side implementation. Calls land here\n// automatically when the model invokes the tool.\nconst lookupContact = lookupContactDef.client((input) => {\n  // Look up in local state, IndexedDB, an in-process address book, etc.\n  return runLookupOnClient(input);\n});\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage, partial, final } = useChat({\n  outputSchema: RecommendationSchema,\n  tools: [lookupContact],\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/recommend\"),\n});\n```\n\nWhile the client tool runs, the agent loop is paused and the structured-output stream hasn't started — `partial` stays `{}` and `final` stays `null`. As soon as the `.client()` implementation returns, the loop resumes, the structured stream takes over, and `partial` / `final` populate.\n\nSee [Client Tools](../tools/client-tools) for the full pattern (typed inputs / outputs, multiple client tools, mixing with server tools, surfacing tool calls in the message renderer).\n\n## Multi-turn + tools + structured output\n\nComposes naturally. Every turn runs the agent loop (with any tool gates), then produces a structured-output assistant message when the run completes successfully. The next turn sees the prior recipe (or recommendation, or report) as assistant content and can iterate on it. The separate-finalization path can also retain the agent loop's plain-text assistant message before that structured response.\n\nThe only thing to be careful of: between `sendMessage()` and the first structured-output event, the latest turn has no `structured-output` part yet — your render loop's `m.parts.find(p => p.type === \"structured-output\")` returns `undefined`. Render a \"streaming…\" placeholder when `isLoading && messages[last]?.role === \"user\"` to cover that gap. See [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn) for the full pattern.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Structured Outputs With Tools\nid: structured-outputs-with-tools\norder: 5\ndescription: \"Combine outputSchema with tools so the agent loop runs first (calling tools as needed) and then returns a validated typed object. Includes the pause / resume flow for tool-approval gates and client-tool invocations inside a structured run.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - structured outputs\n  - tools\n  - agent loop\n  - tool approval\n  - client tools\n  - outputSchema tools\n---\n\nYou want the agent to use tools to gather information, then return a structured object summarizing what it found. \"Recommend a product for a developer\" → the loop calls `getProductPrice`, hits an inventory API, then returns `{ productName, currentPrice, reason }` validated against your schema. The structured response only fires after every tool resolves.\n\nThis page covers the combined `outputSchema` + `tools` shape, including the pause/resume points (server-tool approval prompts, client-tool invocations) that can land mid-run before the structured object arrives.\n\nOn adapters that support native combined mode (modern OpenAI, Claude 4.5+, Gemini 3.x, Grok 4.x, and [OpenRouter on those same upstream models](../adapters/openrouter.md#tools-and-structured-output-together)), `chat({ tools, outputSchema, stream: true })` is one streaming request. The extra finalization call does not run.\n\nThe React chat example has a live OpenRouter page at `/generations/openrouter-combined`.\n\n> **Note:** If you're not yet familiar with how tools work in TanStack AI, read [Tool Architecture](../tools/tool-architecture) and [Server Tools](../tools/server-tools) first. The patterns here build on the regular agent-loop flow — `outputSchema` just adds a final terminal event.\n\n## Non-streaming: tools first, then structured object\n\nThe simplest shape: `await chat({ tools, outputSchema })`. The agent loop runs to completion (every tool resolved, every approval responded to), then the model produces the structured object. The promise resolves with the validated, typed result.\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst getProductPrice = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"get_product_price\",\n  description: \"Get the current price of a product\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ productId: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ productId }) => {\n  return { price: 29.99, currency: \"USD\" };\n});\n\nconst RecommendationSchema = z.object({\n  productName: z.string(),\n  currentPrice: z.number(),\n  reason: z.string(),\n});\n\nconst recommendation = await chat({\n  adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n  messages: [{ role: \"user\", content: \"Recommend a product for a developer\" }],\n  tools: [getProductPrice],\n  outputSchema: RecommendationSchema,\n});\n\nrecommendation.productName;  // string — fully typed\nrecommendation.currentPrice; // number\nrecommendation.reason;       // string\n```\n\nThe agent decides when to call `get_product_price`, executes the tool, integrates the result into its reasoning, and only then produces the final structured response. You see the validated object; the tool calls happen behind the scenes.\n\n> **Note:** Coding agents in a sandbox (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Grok Build) run native tools and the schema on the same turn. They do not pause for TanStack tool approval or client tools. See [Harness Agents](./harnesses).\n\n## Streaming: lifecycle events before the structured payload\n\nPass `stream: true` and the wire format changes — the client now sees tool-call events as they happen, _then_ the structured-output stream emits its terminal event. The lifecycle ordering is:\n\n1. `RUN_STARTED`\n2. (Agent loop) `TOOL_CALL_START` → `TOOL_CALL_ARGS` → `TOOL_CALL_END` → `TOOL_CALL_RESULT`, possibly repeating for multiple tool calls or iterations\n3. `structured-output.start` (once the model begins emitting the JSON response)\n4. `TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT` deltas (the JSON itself)\n5. `structured-output.complete` (completed payload)\n6. `RUN_FINISHED`\n\n`useChat`'s `partial` stays `{}` and `final` stays `null` while step 2 is running — the structured stream hasn't started yet. Once step 3 fires, `partial` begins filling in; on step 5, `final` snaps.\n\nOn the separate-finalization path, the agent loop may also complete a plain-text assistant message before step 3. That message and the structured-output assistant message remain separate. Native-combined output keeps the structured JSON and its part on one assistant message.\n\nThe tool-call parts land on the assistant message exactly as they would in a normal streaming chat. Render them however you'd render tool calls outside a structured-output run.\n\n## Server tools that need approval\n\nA server tool registered with `needsApproval: true` doesn't execute automatically — the agent loop pauses, the queued tool-call lands on the assistant message as a `ToolCallPart` with `state === \"approval-requested\"`, and the loop waits for you to call `addToolApprovalResponse({ id, approved })` from the hook return. The structured-output stream only takes over once approval is granted (or denied and the loop resumes).\n\n```tsx ignore\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { RecommendationSchema } from \"./api/recommend\";\nimport { sendEmail } from \"./tools\";\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage, partial, final, addToolApprovalResponse } =\n  useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/recommend\"),\n    outputSchema: RecommendationSchema,\n    tools: [sendEmail], // server tool with needsApproval: true\n  });\n\nconst last = messages.at(-1);\n\nreturn (\n  <>\n    {last?.parts.map((part, i) => {\n      // Surface approval prompts inline.\n      if (\n        part.type === \"tool-call\" &&\n        part.state === \"approval-requested\" &&\n        part.approval\n      ) {\n        return (\n          <ApprovalPrompt\n            key={i}\n            part={part}\n            onApprove={() =>\n              addToolApprovalResponse({ id: part.approval!.id, approved: true })\n            }\n            onDeny={() =>\n              addToolApprovalResponse({ id: part.approval!.id, approved: false })\n            }\n          />\n        );\n      }\n      if (part.type === \"thinking\") return <ReasoningView key={i} text={part.content} />;\n      if (part.type === \"tool-call\") return <ToolCallView key={i} part={part} />;\n      return null;\n    })}\n\n    {/* The structured payload — fills in once tools resolve. */}\n    <StructuredView data={final ?? partial} />\n  </>\n);\n```\n\nWhile the approval is pending, `partial` stays at its last value (which is `{}` on a first run) and `final` stays `null`. As soon as the user approves (or denies and the loop continues), the agent loop resumes, the structured stream runs, and `partial` / `final` populate.\n\nThe full server-tool approval pattern lives in [Tool Approval Flow](../tools/tool-approval). The only structured-output-specific note: the approval can land **before** the structured stream starts. That's why `partial` reads `{}` while you're staring at an approval prompt — there's no JSON yet.\n\n## Client tools mid-run\n\nClient tools — defined with `.client((input) => ...)` on the tool definition — execute automatically when the model calls them. The server ends the current run with an internal `client-tool-execution` interrupt that does not appear in the public `interrupts` array. The client runs the registered `.client()` implementation and submits its output in a resume batch. Once every client tool resolves, the agent loop continues into the structured-output stream. There's no `onToolCall` option to wire up on the hook side.\n\n```tsx\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { runLookupOnClient } from \"./client-utils\";\nimport { RecommendationSchema } from \"./api/recommend\";\n\nconst lookupContactDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"lookup_contact\",\n  description: \"Find a contact by name\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ name: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ email: z.string(), phone: z.string() }),\n});\n\n// `.client()` registers the browser-side implementation. Calls land here\n// automatically when the model invokes the tool.\nconst lookupContact = lookupContactDef.client((input) => {\n  // Look up in local state, IndexedDB, an in-process address book, etc.\n  return runLookupOnClient(input);\n});\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage, partial, final } = useChat({\n  outputSchema: RecommendationSchema,\n  tools: [lookupContact],\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/recommend\"),\n});\n```\n\nWhile the client tool runs, the agent loop is paused and the structured-output stream hasn't started — `partial` stays `{}` and `final` stays `null`. As soon as the `.client()` implementation returns, the loop resumes, the structured stream takes over, and `partial` / `final` populate.\n\nSee [Client Tools](../tools/client-tools) for the full pattern (typed inputs / outputs, multiple client tools, mixing with server tools, surfacing tool calls in the message renderer).\n\n## Multi-turn + tools + structured output\n\nComposes naturally. Every turn runs the agent loop (with any tool gates), then produces a structured-output assistant message when the run completes successfully. The next turn sees the prior recipe (or recommendation, or report) as assistant content and can iterate on it. The separate-finalization path can also retain the agent loop's plain-text assistant message before that structured response.\n\nThe only thing to be careful of: between `sendMessage()` and the first structured-output event, the latest turn has no `structured-output` part yet — your render loop's `m.parts.find(p => p.type === \"structured-output\")` returns `undefined`. Render a \"streaming…\" placeholder when `isLoading && messages[last]?.role === \"user\"` to cover that gap. See [Multi-Turn Chat](./multi-turn) for the full pattern.\n"}
